#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""close-ticket <issue> <base>..<head> <checkout> [-R owner/repo]

Closes a ticket in seconds and dispatches nothing (spec #160, #165). Fetches `<issue>`'s body,
runs each `## Acceptance criteria` item's own `check:` marker (`bin/ticket_shape.py`'s
`criteria_blocks`/`parse_check_marker`, #166) in `<checkout>`, records the exit status it
observed, renders the `## Closing record`, posts it, and closes the ticket.

A criterion with no marker is prose — today's filing-time behaviour is unchanged — and is
recorded `UNVERIFIED`, never `MET`: a script has no way to judge prose, so it doesn't try. A
criterion whose marker command exits non-zero aborts the whole run before anything is posted:
the failing criterion and the command's combined output go to stderr, nothing is commented,
the issue stays open, and this exits non-zero. There is no partial close and no `needs-human`
stamp on this route — re-running the same invocation after the work is fixed is the entire
recovery path.

Two shapes abort the same way, and for the same reason: **unverified is not a verdict this
script may close on**. Both come from `collod873/claude-workflow`'s #215, whose copy of this
tool grew them first; they are merged back here so the source of truth `bin/re-seed` vendors
out is a superset of what it would overwrite, never a silent revert of it (#194).

- A criterion that says `check:` and hands this script nothing runnable (`UNPARSEABLE_CHECK_RE`)
  is a *failure*, never an `UNVERIFIED: no check` bullet. It was the second of those until
  #215 there: a producer emitted `- [ ] check: grep -q ...` — no delimiter, no backticks — which
  read as prose, so two tickets each closed on `0 of 8 criteria verified` against work nobody had
  checked. A criterion meaning to be checked and failing to say how is a defect in the ticket.
- A ticket whose criteria come back **every criterion unverified** does not close at all. The
  summary line was supposed to make that erosion visible; a record saying `0 of 8` still closed
  the issue, which made it visible to nobody. Zero of any number is not evidence.

A body carrying no `## Acceptance criteria` heading closes on the `No diff.` form, matching a
map or task ticket's existing exit (`ticket_shape.criteria_blocks` returns `None` for that
shape).

The record's wire shape is unchanged from the one 445 existing checker-written records already
use — `hooks/close-gate.py`'s `RECORD_HEADING`, `RANGE_LINE_RE` and `BULLET_RE` parse this
record the same way they parse a human-written one — with one addition: a summary line beneath
the range counting criteria verified vs. unverified, so erosion of the guarantee stays visible
on every ticket rather than inferred.

Checks run with `<checkout>` as their working directory, never this process's own — marker
commands are author-written, and in `/drain` they fire inside the merge lock. Passing a live
work tree as `<checkout>` is the caller's choice to make, not this script's.

`--superseded-by <n>` retires the ticket into an already-open successor instead of running any
checks (#167): emits `Superseded by #<n>` in the slot the range line would occupy, and every
criterion in the body needs a `--moved <where>` or `--dropped <why>` reason supplied at the
invocation, one apiece, in the body's own order — the run refuses before posting anything when
the reasons supplied don't exactly cover the criteria. `<n>`'s state is read with
`gh issue view <n> --json state` through `run_gh` — the same one resolution path every other
`gh` call in this script uses, never a second one — and the run refuses to post or close when
`<n>` doesn't resolve at all (an unresolvable successor) as well as when it resolves but is
already closed (a closed successor): superseding into a closed ticket retires this ticket's
debt instead of moving it. The emitted form satisfies `hooks/close-gate.py`'s
`SUPERSEDED_LINE_RE` and `DISPOSITION_RE`.

`--spec` closes a spec (a `prd` issue) instead of a ticket. It is a mode, not a second script:
`criteria_blocks`, `split_check_marker`, `run_check` and `render_record` are the same functions a
ticket's close runs, so a spec's one criterion is run, recorded and refused inside the same
all-or-nothing contract. What `--spec` adds is a **precondition** and a stricter **verdict**.

The precondition is checked before any check command runs: every sub-issue must be **delivered**
— closed by a *merged* pull request, not merely closed. A child closed as `not planned`, or
closed by hand, is not delivered; the run refuses, names every undelivered child and why, and
posts and closes nothing.

The verdict: a spec's criterion is `MET` only when its check exits 0 **and** prints something on
stdout, and the `MET` bullet carries that stdout (trimmed and capped, quoted by `quote_evidence`)
beneath the command. An exit 0 that printed **no evidence** is a failure, refused down the same
path as a non-zero exit — name the criterion on stderr, post nothing, close nothing. #236 closed
on `gh issue list --label sliceable … | xargs -r …`, whose first half returns `[]` in a world
where nothing was built and whose second half runs nothing and exits 0 on empty input: an unbuilt
world and a proven one scored identically, because this script read the exit status and never
what the check said (#270). Ticket mode is unmoved — `grep -q`, `! grep -q` and `test -f` succeed
in silence by design, and every ticket check written so far relies on that. The rule belongs to
the world-reading check a spec carries, the one place a silent pass can mean nothing happened.

The precondition comes first because a spec's check reads the world: running it against a spec
whose slices have not all landed burns a run on a verdict that could not have been green, and
reports red for a reason that is not the product's fault. A spec with no sub-issues at all has
nothing undelivered and passes the precondition — the gate is "every child delivered", and
vacuous truth is the honest reading of it for a spec that was never sliced.

A spec's criterion is *required* to read the tracker or production, which is the exact opposite
of the rule a published ticket's criterion is held to — see `bin/ticket_shape.py`'s `spec` branch
for why the ban is not extended here.

`gh` is resolved and invoked entirely through `bin/gh_support.py` (`gh_bin`, `bind_gh`,
`run_gh`, `GhError`, `AGENT_SKILLS_GH`) — the one resolution path and one failure envelope
every `gh`-calling tool in this repo shares (#102, #109); there is no second copy here.
"""
import argparse
import json
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
import gh_support  # noqa: E402
try:
    import run_log  # noqa: E402
except ImportError:
    # A vendored copy whose bin/run_log.py never arrived (#183 pairs only files the target
    # repo already carries). Losing this repo's run rows is a gap in an audit; an
    # ImportError here would be a broken tool.
    run_log = None
import ticket_shape  # noqa: E402

NO_DIFF_RECORD = "## Closing record\n\nNo diff.\n"

# A criterion that mentions `check:` but yields no command. Wider than
# `ticket_shape.CHECK_MARKER_ATTEMPT_RE` on purpose: that one requires the delimiter, and the
# shape that actually reached the tracker carried none — so the criteria that producer wrote
# were not *malformed* markers, they were invisible ones, and the run that closed on them
# reported "no check" about a line whose first word is `check`. The looser question — did this
# criterion mean to name a check — is the one worth asking, because a criterion that says
# `check:` and does not parse is a ticket defect either way.
UNPARSEABLE_CHECK_RE = re.compile(r"\bcheck:", re.IGNORECASE)

# A `MET` bullet as this script writes one, anchored to the start of its own line. Counted off
# the rendered record for the run row, so it must not also match the text a spec's check printed
# — `quote_evidence` guarantees no evidence line begins with `- `, and this anchor is the other
# half of that guarantee.
MET_BULLET_RE = re.compile(r"^- .* — MET: ", re.MULTILINE)

# How much of a spec check's stdout the record carries: enough to show what the check saw — an
# issue number, a count, the line that matched — without pasting a build log onto the issue.
EVIDENCE_MAX_LINES = 10
EVIDENCE_MAX_CHARS = 800


def fetch_body(gh, issue: str) -> str:
    out = gh_support.run_gh(gh, "issue", "view", issue, "--json", "body")
    return json.loads(out).get("body") or ""


def fetch_state(gh, issue) -> str:
    """The successor's open/closed state, read the one way this script ever reads a
    successor's liveness — `gh issue view <n> --json state` through `gh_support.run_gh`
    (#167's acceptance criteria: no second `gh` resolution path). Raises `GhError` the same
    way `fetch_body` does when `<n>` doesn't resolve at all — that's what lets `main` tell an
    unresolvable successor apart from one that resolves but is closed."""
    out = gh_support.run_gh(gh, "issue", "view", str(issue), "--json", "state")
    return json.loads(out).get("state") or ""


SUB_ISSUES_QUERY = """
query {
  repository(owner: "%(owner)s", name: "%(name)s") {
    issue(number: %(issue)s) {
      subIssues(first: 100) {
        nodes {
          number
          state
          stateReason
          closedByPullRequestsReferences(first: 20, includeClosedPrs: true) {
            nodes { number merged }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
"""


def fetch_sub_issues(gh_path: str, name_with_owner: str, issue: str) -> list[dict]:
    """Every sub-issue of `issue`, with the state and the pull requests `undelivered` needs.

    `includeClosedPrs: true` because the pull request this is looking for is by definition no
    longer open — a merged one is a closed one, and the default omits it.

    One GraphQL call rather than a REST child list plus a call per child: the question is about
    every child at once, and a spec with a dozen slices should cost one round trip.

    Bound off `gh_path` with no `-R`, unlike every other call here. `bind_gh`'s `repo` appends
    `-R` to the argv, and `gh api graphql` takes no such flag — the repository is named inside
    the query instead. Still the one resolution path (`gh_bin`), which is what #102 and #109
    actually require; a second *binding* of the same resolved binary is not a second way of
    finding `gh`."""
    owner, _, name = name_with_owner.partition("/")
    query = SUB_ISSUES_QUERY % {"owner": owner, "name": name, "issue": issue}
    api = gh_support.bind_gh(gh_path)
    out = gh_support.run_gh(api, "api", "graphql", "-f", f"query={query}")
    issue_node = json.loads(out)["data"]["repository"]["issue"]
    if issue_node is None:
        raise gh_support.GhError(f"issue #{issue} not found")
    return issue_node["subIssues"]["nodes"]


def undelivered(children: list[dict]) -> list[str]:
    """One line per child that has not been delivered, naming the child and why — empty when
    every child has.

    Delivered is *closed by a merged pull request*, never merely closed: the distinction this
    estate already draws for a blocker, applied to a spec's children. An issue closed by hand
    proves someone thought it was done; a merged PR is the only closure that says code landed.
    `not planned` is the case worth naming separately — it is a deliberate non-delivery, and a
    spec whose slice was abandoned should be re-sliced or its criterion rewritten, not closed.

    The question is asked from the *pull request's* side — `closedByPullRequestsReferences`,
    GitHub's own index of `Closes #N` in PR bodies — and never from the child's close event
    (#253). Lane 08 closes each slice itself, with this script, after its own gate (#195), so by
    the time the PR merges the issue is already closed and GitHub's merge-time auto-close never
    fires: every slice this chain delivers carries no pull request on its close event at all.
    Reading that event called all six of #233's merged slices "closed by hand" and left the rule
    #233 exists to introduce unable to close itself. `integrate.ts:127-145` had already ruled
    GitHub's closing linkage untrustworthy and read `Closes #N` out of the PR body instead; this
    asks the same question of the same index."""
    problems = []
    for child in children:
        number = child.get("number")
        if (child.get("state") or "").upper() != "CLOSED":
            problems.append(f"#{number}: still open")
            continue
        if (child.get("stateReason") or "").upper() == "NOT_PLANNED":
            problems.append(f"#{number}: closed as not planned — not delivered")
            continue
        naming = (child.get("closedByPullRequestsReferences") or {}).get("nodes") or []
        if any(pr.get("merged") for pr in naming):
            continue
        if naming:
            unmerged = ", ".join(f"#{pr.get('number')}" for pr in naming)
            problems.append(f"#{number}: closed by PR {unmerged}, which is not merged")
        else:
            problems.append(f"#{number}: closed by hand, not by a merged PR")
    return problems


def split_check_marker(criterion: str) -> tuple[str, str | None]:
    """`(reference, command)` for one folded criterion block. `reference` has the leading
    `- [ ]`/`- [x]` checkbox (`ticket_shape.CRITERIA_ITEM_RE`) stripped — the posted bullet
    supplies its own leading `- `, so keeping the checkbox would double it. `command` is
    `None` when the criterion carries no well-formed `check:` marker — plain prose and a
    malformed marker read alike here, since `ticket_shape.validate` already warns a filer
    about a malformed one at filing time (#166); close-ticket doesn't re-litigate that, it
    only asks whether there is a command to run. When a marker is well-formed, `reference`
    also has it sliced off, using the same `CHECK_MARKER_RE` `parse_check_marker` matched
    against — so the two can never disagree about where the marker starts."""
    criterion = criterion.strip()
    box = ticket_shape.CRITERIA_ITEM_RE.match(criterion)
    if box:
        criterion = criterion[box.end():].strip()
    command = ticket_shape.parse_check_marker(criterion)
    if command is None:
        return criterion, None
    m = ticket_shape.CHECK_MARKER_RE.search(criterion)
    return criterion[: m.start()].rstrip(), command


def run_check(command: str, checkout: str) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """Run one criterion's marker `command` with `checkout` as its cwd — never this
    process's own tree (module docstring)."""
    return subprocess.run(command, shell=True, cwd=checkout, capture_output=True, text=True)


def quote_evidence(output: str) -> list[str]:
    """`output` as record lines that can never be miscounted as a criterion's bullet.

    `hooks/close-gate.py`'s `BULLET_RE` is `^[ \\t]*-\\s+` run over the raw record text — it
    counts bullets before any markdown is rendered, so indenting a fence hides nothing: a `- `
    the check printed still starts its line, and the gate would then see more bullets than the
    body has criteria and deny the close. Only a non-whitespace first character keeps that from
    happening, so every line is prefixed `> `. The fence inside the quote is what keeps the
    output monospaced and its leading whitespace intact, and it is one backtick longer than the
    longest run in the output so a check that prints a fence of its own cannot end this one
    early.

    Trimmed to `EVIDENCE_MAX_CHARS`/`EVIDENCE_MAX_LINES`: the record is evidence that the check
    read a real world, not a transcript of everything it read."""
    text = output.strip()
    if len(text) > EVIDENCE_MAX_CHARS:
        text = text[:EVIDENCE_MAX_CHARS].rstrip() + " […]"
    lines = text.split("\n")
    if len(lines) > EVIDENCE_MAX_LINES:
        lines = lines[:EVIDENCE_MAX_LINES] + ["[…]"]
    longest = max((len(run) for run in re.findall(r"`+", text)), default=0)
    fence = "`" * max(3, longest + 1)
    return [f"> {fence}", *(f"> {line}".rstrip() for line in lines), f"> {fence}"]


def render_record(range_spec: str, blocks: list[str] | None,
                   checkout: str, spec: bool = False) -> tuple[str | None, bool]:
    """Returns `(record_text, ok)`. `ok` is False the moment a criterion's check exits
    non-zero — `record_text` is then `None`, and the failing criterion plus the command's
    combined output have already gone to stderr. The caller must not post or close in that
    case (module docstring).

    `spec` is the `--spec` verdict: a criterion is `MET` only when its check exits 0 *and*
    prints something, and the `MET` bullet then carries that output. A silent exit 0 refuses
    down the same path a non-zero exit does. Ticket mode (`spec=False`) accepts a silent exit 0,
    which is what `grep -q` and `test -f` have always been — see the module docstring for why
    the two modes read the same exit status differently."""
    if blocks is None:
        return NO_DIFF_RECORD, True

    lines = []
    verified = 0
    for criterion in blocks:
        reference, command = split_check_marker(criterion)
        if command is None:
            if UNPARSEABLE_CHECK_RE.search(criterion):
                print(
                    "error: criterion names a check this script cannot run: "
                    f"{reference}",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
                print(
                    "  a runnable criterion reads: <what is observably true> — check: "
                    "`<command>` — one backtick-quoted command, nothing after it",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
                return None, False
            lines.append(f"- {reference} — UNVERIFIED: no check")
            continue
        result = run_check(command, checkout)
        if result.returncode != 0:
            output = (result.stdout or "") + (result.stderr or "")
            print(f"error: check failed for criterion: {reference}", file=sys.stderr)
            print(f"  command: {command}", file=sys.stderr)
            print(f"  exit status: {result.returncode}", file=sys.stderr)
            if output.strip():
                print(output.rstrip("\n"), file=sys.stderr)
            return None, False
        evidence = (result.stdout or "").strip()
        if spec and not evidence:
            print(f"error: check printed no evidence for criterion: {reference}",
                   file=sys.stderr)
            print(f"  command: {command}", file=sys.stderr)
            print(
                "  exit status: 0, stdout empty — a spec's check reads the world, and a "
                "reading that produced nothing is not a reading. Exit 0 against an empty "
                "world and exit 0 against a proven one are the same status; only the output "
                "tells them apart.",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return None, False
        lines.append(f"- {reference} — MET: `{command}` exit 0")
        if spec:
            lines.extend(quote_evidence(evidence))
        verified += 1

    total = len(blocks)
    unverified = total - verified
    if total and not verified:
        print(
            f"error: {total} acceptance criteria and every criterion unverified — nothing "
            "here was checked, so there is nothing to close on",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        print(
            "  give each criterion a trailing — check: `<command>` marker, or close this "
            "as not planned if it is not being delivered",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return None, False
    summary = f"{verified} of {total} criteria verified · {unverified} unverified"
    body = "\n".join([f"`{range_spec}`", "", summary, "", *lines])
    return f"## Closing record\n\n{body}\n", True


def render_superseded_record(successor: int, blocks: list[str] | None,
                              dispositions: list[tuple[str, str]]) -> tuple[str | None, str | None]:
    """Returns `(record_text, error)`. `error` is `None`, and `record_text` is the rendered
    `## Closing record`, only when `dispositions` — the `--moved`/`--dropped` reasons supplied
    at the invocation, in the order they were given — has exactly one entry per criterion in
    `blocks`. Otherwise `record_text` is `None` and `error` names the mismatch; the caller must
    not post or close in that case, matching `render_record`'s all-or-nothing contract.

    Each bullet pairs one of `blocks` with one of `dispositions`, in order — the body's own
    criteria order, since `--moved`/`--dropped` are meant to be typed in that same order — and
    is rendered `- <reference> — MOVED: <where>` or `- <reference> — DROPPED: <why>`, the exact
    slot and em dash `hooks/close-gate.py`'s (dead-pending-#168) `DISPOSITION_RE` matches. The
    line above the bullets is `Superseded by #<successor>`, alone on its own line — the slot the
    range line occupies elsewhere, and the shape `SUPERSEDED_LINE_RE` matches. `blocks is None`
    (no `## Acceptance criteria` heading at all) needs zero dispositions and renders no bullets,
    the same "declares no criteria" shape `close-gate.py`'s `evaluate_record` already excuses."""
    required = len(blocks) if blocks is not None else 0
    if len(dispositions) != required:
        return None, (
            f"error: {required} acceptance criteria in the body but {len(dispositions)} "
            "--moved/--dropped reasons supplied — exactly one reason per criterion, in the "
            "body's own order."
        )
    bullets = []
    for criterion, (kind, reason) in zip(blocks or [], dispositions):
        reference, _ = split_check_marker(criterion)
        bullets.append(f"- {reference} — {kind}: {reason}")
    lines = [f"Superseded by #{successor}"]
    if bullets:
        lines += ["", *bullets]
    return "## Closing record\n\n" + "\n".join(lines) + "\n", None


class DispositionAction(argparse.Action):
    """Appends `(kind, value)` to the shared `dispositions` list, `kind` being `MOVED` or
    `DROPPED` depending on which of the two flags fired. Both `--moved` and `--dropped` are
    registered against this one action with the same `dest`, so whichever order they're typed
    on the command line is the order this list ends up in — argparse invokes an action the
    moment it consumes that flag's argument, in argv order, regardless of flag identity — which
    is what lets `render_superseded_record` zip this list against the body's own criteria order
    without a second, separate ordering scheme."""

    def __call__(self, parser, namespace, values, option_string=None):
        kind = "MOVED" if option_string == "--moved" else "DROPPED"
        getattr(namespace, self.dest).append((kind, values))


def parse_args(argv):
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="close-ticket", description=__doc__,
                                      formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    parser.add_argument("issue")
    parser.add_argument("range", metavar="BASE..HEAD")
    parser.add_argument("checkout")
    parser.add_argument("-R", "--repo")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--spec", action="store_true",
        help="Close a spec (a `prd` issue) rather than a ticket: refuse unless every sub-issue "
             "is delivered (closed by a merged PR), then run the spec's one check as usual.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--superseded-by", type=int, metavar="N", default=None,
        help="Retire this ticket against already-open issue #N instead of running any checks; "
             "requires --moved/--dropped for every criterion in the body.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--moved", action=DispositionAction, dest="dispositions", default=[], metavar="WHERE",
        help="This criterion's work now lives WHERE (e.g. '#152 criterion 2'). Repeatable, "
             "one per criterion, in the body's own order; mixes freely with --dropped.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--dropped", action=DispositionAction, dest="dispositions", metavar="WHY",
        help="This criterion is not being carried forward, WHY. Repeatable, one per criterion, "
             "in the body's own order; mixes freely with --moved.",
    )
    return parser.parse_args(argv)


ROW: dict = {}


def main(argv=None) -> int:
    """`run()` plus its run row (#182): one per invocation, verdict `closed` or `refused`,
    carrying the issue, the criterion count and how many of them a check actually verified.

    A wrapper rather than a call at each `return 1` because there are nine of them and a
    gate whose record depends on nobody forgetting one is the shape this ticket exists to
    end. `ROW` is filled in as `run()` learns each field, so a refusal still records
    everything known before it refused."""
    ROW.clear()
    code = run(argv)
    if run_log is not None:
        run_log.append_log("closed" if code == 0 else "refused", **ROW)
    return code


def run(argv=None) -> int:
    args = parse_args(argv)
    ROW["issue"] = args.issue

    base, sep, head = args.range.partition("..")
    if not sep or not base or not head:
        print(f"error: range must be BASE..HEAD, got {args.range!r}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    gh_path = gh_support.gh_bin()
    if not gh_path:
        print(
            "error: gh not found (set AGENT_SKILLS_GH to point at a gh binary)",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        return 1
    gh = gh_support.bind_gh(gh_path, args.repo)

    try:
        body = fetch_body(gh, args.issue)
    except gh_support.GhError as e:
        print(f"error: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    blocks = ticket_shape.criteria_blocks(body)
    ROW["criteria"] = 0 if blocks is None else len(blocks)

    if args.superseded_by is not None:
        try:
            state = fetch_state(gh, args.superseded_by)
        except gh_support.GhError as e:
            print(
                f"error: successor #{args.superseded_by} could not be resolved: {e}",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return 1
        if state.upper() != "OPEN":
            print(
                f"error: successor #{args.superseded_by} is not open "
                f"(state: {state or 'unknown'}) — superseding into a closed ticket retires "
                "this ticket's debt instead of moving it; name an open issue",
                file=sys.stderr,
            )
            return 1
        record_text, error = render_superseded_record(args.superseded_by, blocks,
                                                        args.dispositions)
        if error is not None:
            print(error, file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
    else:
        if args.spec:
            # Before `render_record`, which is where check commands actually run — the whole
            # point of the precondition is that a spec whose slices have not landed never
            # spends a run on a verdict that could not have been green.
            try:
                name_with_owner = json.loads(gh_support.run_gh(
                    gh, "repo", "view", "--json", "nameWithOwner"))["nameWithOwner"]
                children = fetch_sub_issues(gh_path, name_with_owner, args.issue)
            except (gh_support.GhError, KeyError, ValueError) as e:
                print(f"error: could not read #{args.issue}'s sub-issues: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
                return 1
            ROW["children"] = len(children)
            problems = undelivered(children)
            if problems:
                print(
                    f"error: {len(problems)} of {len(children)} sub-issues not delivered — a "
                    "spec closes on its own check, but only once every slice beneath it has "
                    "actually landed:",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
                for problem in problems:
                    print(f"  {problem}", file=sys.stderr)
                return 1
        record_text, ok = render_record(args.range, blocks, args.checkout, spec=args.spec)
        if not ok:
            return 1

    # Counted off the record rather than threaded out of `render_record`: the record is
    # what actually gets posted, so counting it is counting what was claimed. Anchored to the
    # start of a line (`MET_BULLET_RE`) rather than matched anywhere in the text, because a
    # spec's record now also carries what its check printed, and a check is free to print
    # anything.
    ROW["verified"] = len(MET_BULLET_RE.findall(record_text))

    try:
        gh_support.run_gh(gh, "issue", "comment", args.issue, "--body-file", "-",
                           input=record_text)
        gh_support.run_gh(gh, "issue", "close", args.issue)
    except gh_support.GhError as e:
        print(f"error: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    sys.stdout.write(record_text)
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
